```java
public interface Scheduler {
    Handle schedule(Runnable task, Trigger trigger);
    void cancel(Handle handle);
}

public interface Trigger {
    Instant nextFireTime(Instant now);
}
```

Separating `Trigger` from the task itself means the same `Scheduler` and worker loop support fixed intervals, cron expressions, or one-off delayed execution — they're all just different `Trigger` implementations.

```java
public class SimpleScheduler implements Scheduler {
    private final PriorityQueue<ScheduledTask> heap =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparing(ScheduledTask::nextFireTime));

    void loop() {
        while (running) {
            ScheduledTask next = heap.peek();
            sleepUntil(next.nextFireTime());
            heap.poll();
            runAsync(next.task());
            reschedule(next);
        }
    }
}
```

The loop only ever sleeps until the single soonest deadline in the entire heap — adding the thousandth scheduled task doesn't slow down finding what's next, since the heap keeps the minimum at the top in logarithmic time per insert.

```java
void reschedule(ScheduledTask t) {
    Instant next = t.trigger().nextFireTime(clock.now());
    if (next.isBefore(clock.now())) {
        next = policy == OverrunPolicy.SKIP
            ? clock.now().plus(t.interval())
            : clock.now();
    }
    heap.add(t.withNextFireTime(next));
}
```

A task that takes 90 seconds on a 60-second interval will have already missed its next scheduled tick by the time it finishes — the policy decides whether to skip straight to the *next* interval boundary or fire again immediately to catch up, both legitimate, explicit choices rather than an accident of the reschedule math.
